  "slug": "adapted-ectotherm-evolve-7afc",
  "summary": "Recent Drosophila preprints link artificial selection, diet and gene disruption to sleep, lifespan and recombination phenotypes.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints examine how selection, genetic background and nutrition shape phenotypic responses in Drosophila melanogaster, with sex-specific effects recurring across them. Artificially selected early and late eclosion chronotype lines show correlated divergence in sleep architecture, fragmentation and recovery. Disrupting the ecdysone oxidase gene fezzik alters transcription, metabolism, L-dopa levels and sex-specific lifespan, with chinmo implicated in female effects. Experimental evolution under yeast deprivation detected no change in male fitness components, suggesting that nutrient-driven adaptation is female-specific. DGRP lines on varying caloric diets show genotype-dependent plasticity in X-chromosome recombination that is not associated with survival or fecundity. Taken together, the items suggest that trait responses can be decoupled from fitness readouts and are often sex-specific, although the studies address distinct traits and do not share a single mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
